{"case_concurrency": 2, "case_id": "diagnosisarena:211", "dataset_id": "diagnosisarena", "dataset_selection_sha256": "13f19340893b04d11fb7eb336f2f9d51370e8ac4818d7205e41728f81503e009", "dataset_snapshot_sha256": "bdf63ade2d1a892e9e3abe9d41c70cfbf62e414989a289dd8e319b339e66f2f5", "model_hash": "e5466c735d57bd3e32d4607a3e372b1862579edfef7864ca514e709a38853e26", "profile_id": "B2", "response": {"answer_text": "```json\n{\n  \"single_choice\": \"A\"\n}\n```\n\n**Rationale:**  \nThe clinical and laboratory findings are consistent with **primary hyperparathyroidism**, including hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, and elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH). The presence of a **brown tumor** (also known as an osteoclastoma) in the distal phalanx, along with radiographic findings of bone resorption and lysis, supports the diagnosis of **osteitis fibrosa cystica**. The histopathology of the tumor shows features of a brown tumor, including fibrous tissue, woven bone, osteolytic cysts, and multinucleated giant cells. These findings are characteristic of **brown tumors secondary to hyperparathyroidism**, which is a complication of **primary hyperparathyroidism**. While **parathyroid carcinoma** is a rare possibility, the clinical presentation and imaging findings are more consistent with **primary hyperparathyroidism with osteitis fibrosa cystica and brown tumor**.", "citations": [], "input_tokens": 1725, "latency_ms": 27176.621, "memory_mode": "off", "model_variant": "qwen3_8b_base", "output_tokens": 1134, "parsed_answer": null, "provider_calls": 3, "reasoning_mode": "adaptive_mdt", "retrieval_mode": "off", "safety_flags": [], "system_profile": "B2", "tool_calls": 0, "trace_id": "harness-cd3b395f956843b495e9627ad9e40696"}, "run_identity": "8e0e440bd0a891bea9dcd52fa2d9d8a9853997938e09c427564f3d0f0be9c164", "score": {"case_id": "diagnosisarena:211", "category": "unknown", "correct": false, "metric": "exact_choice_accuracy", "parse_success": false}, "source_revision": "64bb873fe651e1c71c